<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rennes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Condate Riedonum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rennes is the capital of Brittany in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at the confluence of the Ille and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilaine">Vilaine</a>. It began as Condate, chief town of the Gaulish Redones, and became a Roman civitas capital. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its counts contested the Breton ducal title with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> and the dukes were crowned in its cathedral; after the union with France it housed the Parliament of Brittany, created in 1554.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rennes.jpg" alt="Rennes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_de_la_Mairie,_Rennes,_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brittany, the Armorican peninsula settled by migrants from south-western Britain in the fifth and sixth centuries, had formed a kingdom under Nominoe, Erispoe and Salomon in the ninth century, when its rulers fought the Carolingians and won the counties of <b>Rennes</b> and Nantes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Angers that followed, Charles recognised Erispoe as ruler of the Bretons, with <b>Rennes</b> and Nantes.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Waroch repeatedly raided Frankish territory around <b>Rennes</b> and Nantes and defeated or bought off Frankish armies sent by Guntram and Chilperic I in the 570s and 580s.</blockquote><a href="/state/broerec">Broërec</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Erispoe won again at Jengland in 851, and the Treaty of Angers recognised him as king with <b>Rennes</b>, Nantes and the Pays de Retz.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brittany">Kingdom of Brittany</a></li><li><blockquote>Their oppidum of <b>Condate</b> stood at the confluence of the Ille and the Vilaine, as its Gaulish name, meaning confluence, indicates.</blockquote><a href="/state/redones">Redones</a></li></ul></section>
